media: dw9807-vcm: let the subdev outlive the unbind

Unbinding this driver while a file descriptor is open on its /dev/v4l-subdevN
oopses the kernel when that descriptor is closed:

    Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address 001f01405800006a
    Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000004 [#1]  SMP
    pc : subdev_close+0x30/0xc0 [videodev]
    Call trace:
      subdev_close -> v4l2_release -> __fput -> ____fput
      task_work_run -> do_exit -> do_group_exit -> get_signal

and then "Fixing recursive fault but reboot is needed!", which wedges the
device until the watchdog pulls it out. Reproduced on a Xiaomi POCO X3 NFC
(SM7150) on 2026-09-05; the analysis, with the disassembly, is in
docs/CAMERA-07 of the surya port.

WHY IT HAPPENS

struct dw9807_device is devm_kzalloc'd and the v4l2_subdev is embedded in it, so
unbind frees the subdev. It does not close the descriptors anybody already had
open on the device node, nor invalidate them. On close, subdev_close() reads
sd->internal_ops->close from freed memory: dw9807_int_ops declares .close at
offset 32, which is exactly the `ldr x8, [x8, #32]` the trap lands on, and the
address read back is in the linear map rather than the module range -- it is
data, not code, which is why CFI catches it. An open fd does pin the module, but
unbind does not look at that count: devm frees the struct regardless.

THE FIX

The v4l2 core already has the mechanism. v4l2_device_release_subdev_node() is
the device node's release callback and runs when the LAST reference to the node
is dropped -- after the last close -- and from there it calls
internal_ops->release(). A driver that frees its subdev there cannot be freed
underneath an open descriptor.

So: plain kzalloc instead of devm, and .release frees it. The struct then lives
exactly as long as something can still reach it, which is the invariant that was
missing.

Signed-off-by: Dr. Utsugi <cidwel@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/media/i2c/dw9807-vcm.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/dw9807-vcm.c
@@ -140,9 +140,23 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Called by the v4l2 core when the last reference to the device node is
+ * dropped, which is after the last descriptor on it has been closed. Freeing
+ * here -- and NOT with devm -- is what keeps an unbind from pulling the subdev
+ * out from under a descriptor somebody still holds.
+ */
+static void dw9807_release(struct v4l2_subdev *sd)
+{
+	struct dw9807_device *dw9807_dev = sd_to_dw9807_vcm(sd);
+
+	kfree(dw9807_dev);
+}
+
 static const struct v4l2_subdev_internal_ops dw9807_int_ops = {
 	.open = dw9807_open,
 	.close = dw9807_close,
+	.release = dw9807_release,
 };
 
 static const struct v4l2_subdev_ops dw9807_ops = { };
@@ -180,8 +194,13 @@
 	struct dw9807_device *dw9807_dev;
 	int rval;
 
-	dw9807_dev = devm_kzalloc(&client->dev, sizeof(*dw9807_dev),
-				  GFP_KERNEL);
+	/*
+	 * NOT devm: the subdev is embedded here and has to outlive the unbind,
+	 * because descriptors open on the device node still reach it. It is
+	 * freed in dw9807_release(), which the core calls when the last one
+	 * goes away.
+	 */
+	dw9807_dev = kzalloc(sizeof(*dw9807_dev), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (dw9807_dev == NULL)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
@@ -212,6 +231,7 @@
 err_cleanup:
 	v4l2_ctrl_handler_free(&dw9807_dev->ctrls_vcm);
 	media_entity_cleanup(&dw9807_dev->sd.entity);
+	kfree(dw9807_dev);
 
 	return rval;
 }
